title: Moving a repository
tags: Platform
kind: guide
summary: A repository moves with its files — to another directory, another machine, a drive mounted elsewhere; only an external .metafolder with an absolute root needs it edited by hand.

Nothing in a repository's store names an absolute path: every tracked path is relative to
the root ([[Path representation]]). Only `config.json` records the root, and how it is
found again depends on where `.metafolder/` lives.

!! Standard form

`.metafolder/` inside the root: move, copy or re-mount the whole directory, then load it
where it is now.

```
mf -n music repo unload           # on the daemon that had it, if any
mf repo load /run/media/me/Drive/Music
```

The root is wherever `.metafolder/` is: `config.json` records it as `.`, a path relative
to the directory holding `.metafolder/`, so nothing in the repository names where it
is. A drive mounted at `/media/Drive` on one machine and `/run/media/me/Drive` on
another is the same repository on both, and a load by `--metafolder` finds the root as well as a
load by root does.

A repository created before the relative root holds an absolute one. A load by root
corrects it when it has changed, and writes `.` in its place.

!! External `.metafolder/`

When `.metafolder/` is elsewhere (`--metafolder`), the load reads the root from
`config.json` and cannot guess a new one. After moving the files, edit `root` in
`.metafolder/config.json` (an absolute path) while the repository is unloaded, then load
it as usual:

```
mf repo load --metafolder <path>
```

`mf repo init --metafolder` writes an absolute `root`. A relative one is accepted there
too, resolved against the directory holding `.metafolder/`: with `base/meta` as the
`.metafolder/` and `base/data` as the files, `"root": "data"` lets `base` move as a
whole. The daemon keeps a relative `root` as written when it rewrites `config.json`.

!! Things to know

* The repository keeps its uuid and its name; a load is refused while another loaded repository has the same name ([[Creating and loading a repository]]).
* Case sensitivity is probed again at the load, so a repository moved to a filesystem of the other kind follows it ([[Case sensitivity]]).
* The store must not be on a network filesystem ([[Network and special filesystems]]).
* Moving the files does not change their metadata: the metarecords are the same, and nothing is reconciled on its own. A `mf reconcile` after a copy that did not preserve modification times refreshes them.
